24. Important security issue with AllowAnonymous

I noticed your existing controllers use:

[AllowAnonymous]

for user and vehicle operations. Your supplied code contains this pattern repeatedly.

For Department-based authorization, this is potentially dangerous.

If the application already has authentication globally and [AllowAnonymous] is intentional for an internal reason, you can retain it temporarily.

But production authorization should ideally be:

[Authorize]

rather than:

[AllowAnonymous]

Especially for:

AddUser
UpdateUser
DeleteUser
CreateEstimate
DeleteVehicle

The Department restriction should be enforced server-side regardless.

25. One more improvement — never trust posted Department for users

For User Management, it is okay to post:

Department = BUS

or:

Department = STREETCAR

because Admin/SuperAdmin is intentionally allowed to edit it.

But for VehicleList:

do not allow the browser to decide the Department.

Bad:

Department = vehicleViewModel.Department

Good:

Department = departmentFromUsersTable

or even better:

INSERT ...
SELECT ..., u.Department
FROM SBCES.Users u
WHERE u.UserADId = @UserADId
26. Final architecture

The resulting data model is:

SBCES.Users
────────────────────────────
UserID
UserADId
UserName
RoleId
Department
           │
           │ Department at creation
           ▼
SBCES.VehicleList
────────────────────────────
VehicleListId
VehSeriesCode
ProjDesc
EnteredBy
Department
...

Example:

Users

jsmith       BUS
adoe         STREETCAR

Creates:

VehicleList

Estimate 1001    jsmith    BUS
Estimate 1002    adoe      STREETCAR
Estimate 1003    jsmith    BUS
Estimate 1004    adoe      STREETCAR

Then:

Logged-in user	Estimate	Delete
BUS	BUS	✅
BUS	STREETCAR	❌
STREETCAR	STREETCAR	✅
STREETCAR	BUS	❌

And importantly, this remains true even if somebody bypasses the Kendo UI and manually POSTs:

DeleteVehicle(1002)

The SQL:

DELETE FROM SBCES.VehicleList
WHERE VehicleListId = @VehicleListId
  AND Department = @Department;

prevents the unauthorized deletion.

One change I strongly recommend to your existing code

Your current AddUser/UpdateUser implementation contains SQL string concatenation such as:

"where RoleName = '" + userViewModel.RoleName + "'";

and:

" ,UserName = '" + userViewModel.UserName + "'"

as shown in the supplied controller.

Since we're touching this code anyway, use:

const string sql = @"
    SELECT RoleId
    FROM SBCES.Roles
    WHERE RoleName = @RoleName;
";

userViewModel.RoleId =
    await _dbConnection.QuerySingleOrDefaultAsync<int>(
        sql,
        new
        {
            userViewModel.RoleName
        });

That is safer and also makes the new Department implementation consistent with production Dapper practices.

The database migration + User Department + VehicleList Department + server-side delete restriction are the essential pieces. The Kendo hiding is only a usability layer; it must not be treated as the security mechanism.
Yes. For _Layout.cshtml, I recommend displaying it as:

John Smith | Administrator | BUS

or, for example:

John Smith | User | STREETCAR

The cleanest approach is to expose Department alongside the existing user/role information through the same mechanism your layout already uses.

_Layout.cshtml

If your current layout has something similar to:

<span class="user-name">
    @User.Identity?.Name
</span>

and you already have the role available through User.IsInRole(...), you can use:

@{
    var displayUserName = User.Identity?.Name ?? string.Empty;

    var displayRole =
        User.IsInRole("Admin") ? "Admin" :
        User.IsInRole("SuperAdmin") ? "SuperAdmin" :
        User.IsInRole("User") ? "User" :
        string.Empty;

    var displayDepartment =
        ViewBag.Department as string ?? string.Empty;
}

Then:

<div class="user-info">
    <span class="user-name">
        @displayUserName
    </span>

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(displayRole))
    {
        <span class="user-role">
            @displayRole
        </span>
    }

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(displayDepartment))
    {
        <span class="user-department">
            @displayDepartment
        </span>
    }
</div>

However, I would not recommend ViewBag.Department in _Layout.cshtml, because the layout is rendered by many different controllers/actions and you'd have to populate it everywhere.

Better approach: View Component / claims

Since Department is a user-level property like Role, it should ideally be available as an authentication claim.

When the user logs in, add:

new Claim("Department", user.Department)

Then _Layout.cshtml becomes very simple:

@using System.Security.Claims

@{
    var userName = User.Identity?.Name ?? "";

    var role = User.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.Role)?.Value ?? "";

    var department =
        User.FindFirst("Department")?.Value ?? "";
}

Display:

<div class="user-info">
    <span class="user-name">@userName</span>

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(role))
    {
        <span class="user-role">@role</span>
    }

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(department))
    {
        <span class="user-department">@department</span>
    }
</div>

For nicer presentation:

<div class="user-info">
    <span class="user-name">@userName</span>

    <span class="user-role">
        @role
    </span>

    <span class="user-department">
        @(department == "STREETCAR" ? "StreetCar" : "Bus")
    </span>
</div>
